NAME

ulibSendNodeMessage - Sends a node message to a user on a Xenolink node


SYNOPSIS

UBYTE ulibSendNodeMessage(struct XenolinkNodeMsg *Msg)
                                       a0


DESCRIPTION

This function sends a node message to a Xenolink node.

The type of message is specified by the <MessageType> field.  The following
types of messages may be used by third party applications:


   NODEMSG_TEXT_ARRAY

      Causes Xenolink to send a small node message to the user on a particular
      node.

      The system will say the message is from whatever string you copy into
      the <NodeMsg->OrigUser> field.  You must also fill out the
      <NodeMsg->OrigNode> field, which specifies from which node the message
      came.

      The <NodeMsg->DestNode> field specifies the node to which the message
      will be sent.

      The text to be sent should be copied to the <NodeMsg->TextData> field.
      Note that this field can store only 128 bytes; to send longer messages,
      use the NODEMSG_TEXT_MEMORY command (see further on below).

      The message will be displayed to the user in a format similar to the
      following (bear in mind that the node message header text can be
      configured by the Sysop):

      xxx (on node yyy) sent the following node message:
      : --- text data ---

      For example:

      Jonathan Forbes (on node 2) sent the following node message:
      : If you see an onion ring, answer it!


      Or:

      T. Earl Grey (on node 34) sent the following node message:
      : You summoned me, Captain?



   NODEMSG_TEXT_MEMORY

      This message type is virtually identical to the above type, except
      that the message text is a null-terminated string pointed to by the
      <NodeMsg->VoidData1> field.

      The advantage of this method is that it will permit multiple lines of
      text to be sent, whereas the above method will not.

      Unlike the above message type, this type will NOT provide a header
      line (such as the first line below this one).

      Sayonara V1.0 (on node 0) sent the following node message:
      : Prepare for imminent explusion; see ya later, hoser!

      With this message type, you must build the whole message yourself.  For
      example:

      strcpy(MyNodeMsg, "\
      SpontaneousQuoter V2.0 says that on the day a BBS program faster than\r\n\
      Xenolink is created, the Devil will be seen ice-skating to work.\r\n");

      This message would be relayed verbatim to the user online on the
      desired destination node.

      Note that Xenolink will use the following command to deallocate this
      memory for you when it has sent the message:

         FreeMem(NodeMsg->VoidData1, strlen(NodeMsg->VoidData1)+1);

      Therefore, you must use the AllocMem() function (and not malloc() or any
      other memory function) to allocate enough memory for your node message
      (the length of the string plus one, for the null terminator).

      Also note that this command does NOT require you to fill out
      <NodeMsg->OrigNode> or <NodeMsg->OrigUser>.  It is up to you to provide
      sufficient information to the user of your message's origins, in the
      message text.


   IMMEDIATEMSG_SHUTDOWN

      No longer supported; use the XKILL utility


   IMMEDIATEMSG_KICKOFF

      No longer supported; use the XKILL utility


Xenolink will ignore any node messages with invalid <MessageType> fields.

This function does nothing more than add the node message to a Xenolink node's
message queue; the message will only be displayed when convenient (after a
prompt, for example).

After the node message is displayed, it is freed by Xenolink with the following
function call:

   FreeMem(NodeMsg, sizeof(struct XenolinkNodeMsg));

It is important to note that AllocMem() MUST be used to allocate your node
message; you CANNOT simply pass the address of a local variable
(struct XenolinkNodeMsg) allocated on the stack.

If a user is not online on the destination node, or the destination node is
not currently running, then this function will return an error.  In this
situation, you will still have to FreeMem() your message text if you used
the NODEMSG_TEXT_MEMORY command.


RETURNS

0        - Success
Non-zero - Failure

              1 - Destination node not found
              2 - User not online
